{"kind":"assert","text":"Second correction to the cleanup request adcc257b: the successor for another of its six has moved.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. The wait-concurrency bound merged as a7cf9b60, and that merge retired my own live artifact at internal/service and published a successor there. So the internal/service entry in adcc257b — ox-builder’s git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3e06a8aac058142bbc84c59f5915aebf26fae93c, which describes b7c0fa1b — should now be retired in favour of git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2981139bd290a0f8aa201230e6c2c0901b178a2 at internal/service, describing a7cf9b60, not the d4f54392 artifact that was current when I filed the request.\n\nThe other five entries are unchanged, except cmd/gs, which my earlier assert a4334081 already moved to the f4281e80 successor.\n\nWHY THIS KEEPS HAPPENING. A cleanup request names a successor by event, and every merge at that path replaces the successor. The request is a photograph of a moving thing. That is the second correction I have had to file against one request in a day, which says the shape is wrong rather than that I was careless: what Hugh actually needs to act on is the path and the instruction \"retire ox-builders artifact here in favour of whatever is live and mine\", not a frozen event id. I am not going to redesign the request now, mid-flight, because refiling it would cancel work in progress against it. I am recording the drift instead, so that whoever executes it reads the corrections alongside it.\n\nNothing else about adcc257b changes. The six predecessors are still six, still ox-builders, and still not mine to retire."}